{# ONE GROUPED DROPDOWN OF THE HOUSE FILTER BAR.

   A CLOSED CHIP THAT OPENS A PANEL OF OPTIONS WITH LIVE COUNTS — the
   incidents pattern, which is the standing rule for every filter bar in the
   product.

   IT IS A `<details>`, AND EVERY OPTION IS A LINK. The address is the state,
   so a filtered list is a link somebody can send and a page a browser can go
   back to; and the whole control opens, chooses and clears with no script of
   ours, which is what keeps a filter bar working on a page that has none.

   THE COUNTS ARE OF THE WHOLE SET, not of what is already filtered: a count
   that moved as you filtered could not tell you what picking an option would
   do, which is the one thing it is for. A NOUGHT IS DRAWN — an option that
   vanished when it emptied could not be told from one that never existed. #}
{# THE CLOSED CHIP READS THE CHOSEN OPTION'S LABEL, not its value: an area is
   picked by identifier and read as a name. #}
{% set chosenOption = chosen is null ? null : options|filter(option => option.value == chosen)|first %}
<details class="i-dd">
    <summary class="mchip i-ddt{{ chosen is not null ? ' on' }}">
        <span class="i-ddval">{{ chosenOption ? chosenOption.label : all }}</span>
        <span class="i-ddcaret">&#9662;</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="i-ddmenu">
        <span class="i-ddhead">{{ label }}</span>
        <a class="i-ddopt{{ chosen is null ? ' on' }}"
           href="{{ path(app.request.attributes.get('_route'), query.with(key, null)) }}">
            <span class="i-ddopt-l">{{ all }}</span>
        </a>
        <span class="i-ddsep"></span>
        {% for option in options %}
            <a class="i-ddopt{{ chosen == option.value ? ' on' }}"
               href="{{ path(app.request.attributes.get('_route'), query.with(key, option.value)) }}">
                <span class="i-ddopt-l">{{ option.label }}</span>
                <span class="i-ddopt-n">{{ option.count }}</span>
            </a>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>
</details>
